WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.660
This is a traditional “alioli” recipe.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.780
Traditional as we use to eat nowadays,

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:13.300
the real traditional “alioli” would be only with garlic and olive oil,

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.400
but here we are going to make a trap

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.240
and we'll add egg yolk.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.260
this egg yolk is used

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:27.820
to put less garlic.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:29.520
We take a garlic clove and

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:31.560
we take out the inside part

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.040
and put it into the mortar

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.760
For one egg yolk we’ll use two garlic cloves.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:45.960
We open it

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:48.420
and it's ready.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:55.720
It is important,

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.960
to put a little salt to the garlic

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.440
we put a bit of salt

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:10.720
we need to mash the garlic cloves until getting a paste.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:12.340
We’ll take the egg

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:14.960
We’ll take the egg and

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:17.580
And this is a trick

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.160
see, we have the egg yolk inside.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:24.000
We add the yolk here,

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:27.200
you didn’t know about this trick, did you?

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:29.720
and we add the egg yolk

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:32.400
and

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:33.540
We can start now

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.500
One of the tricks that will help is adding

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:38.800
a little hot water

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:42.400
a few drops of hot water when necessary.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:45.000
We’ll start to whip the eggs.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:49.300
A good virgin olive oil,

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.200
with low acidity, only 0’4 degrees of acidity,

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.740
here we have a Siurana, a catalan olive oil.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:58.140
And we’ll start,

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:00.900
first we’ll start very slow

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.500
and after we’ll keep doing.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:10.260
DAVID WEITZ: So an emulsion is drops of
an immiscible fluid in a second fluid.

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:12.540
Drops of one fluid in a second fluid.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:16.230
A foam is the same thing, except
instead of using a fluid on the inside

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:19.330
of the drops, you use air, a gas.

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:20.710
So how do you make an emulsion?

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:24.070
Well, the best way I can show you is
to do it with this little experiment.

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:27.910
Here I have olive oil and water, and
I'm going to try to mix them together.

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:31.100
And olive oil is not miscible in water.

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:34.690
And when I pour the olive oil
onto the water, it floats on top.

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:36.440
It's lighter than the water.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.630
So the only way I can mix
them is to shake them.

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:40.840
So let me shake them like this.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.720
And look, they do mix, but
they don't completely mix.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:47.960
Instead, you form drops
of the oil in the water.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:49.494
And that's an emulsion.

00:02:49.494 --> 00:02:51.160
But let's watch this for a little while.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:52.900
We put it on the side, and remember.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.150
The oil is lighter than
the water, and it's

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:57.300
going to start to float to the top.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:00.880
And as it floats, the drops
become closer and closer.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.950
And they begin to join together.

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:04.540
And they lose their stability.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:06.970
The emulsion doesn't
remain drops of one fluid

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:09.640
in another but rather phase separates.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:12.080
Becomes the two fluids again.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:14.410
And if you look at them,
if we watch them over time,

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:16.330
you'd see they completely
phase separate.

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:18.500
So this emulsion is not stable.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.230
So how do we make an emulsion?

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:24.910
An emulsion is mixture of one
fluid in a second by making drops.

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:28.660
For example, in this case,
we've made oil drops in water.

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:31.630
The oil drops are now
called the dispersed phase.

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:34.670
The water is called
the continuous phase.

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:37.450
And to make an emulsion, you
have to mix them together.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:41.020
Somehow you have to form
drops of the dispersed phase

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.650
in the continuous phase.

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:46.660
And when you do that, you get these
drops, and that's your emulsion.

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:49.630
But to make them stable, you
have to prevent the drops,

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:53.200
or you have to keep the
drops as individual drops.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.680
You can't allow them to
join together or coalesce.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:00.340
Somehow you have to
stabilize the interface

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:04.220
of the drops of the oil in the water.

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:06.685
And we do that using a surfactant.

00:04:06.685 --> 00:04:10.430
A surfactant is a
surface active molecule.

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:12.610
It's an amphophilic molecule.

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:15.640
Part of it is water-like,
or hydrophilic,

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:18.793
and the other part is
oil-like, or hydrophobic.

00:04:18.793 --> 00:04:19.959
And they're joined together.

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:25.240
So the surfactant molecules
like both the oil and the water,

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:27.130
and they can't really
decide which they want.

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:31.090
So they can't really sit in
either the oil or the water.

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:34.360
Instead, they prefer to
sit at the interface,

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:37.660
at the surface between the two fluids.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:42.050
Typically, you put the surfactant
in the continuous phase.

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:47.330
So if you have an oil in water emulsion,
you put the surfactant in the water.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:51.020
So part of the surfactant
sits at the oil.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:52.810
There's excess surfactant, however.

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:56.725
You need to have excess surfactant
so that you always have more

00:04:56.725 --> 00:04:59.920
surfactant available, so that
it can come to the interface

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:03.130
as you create the interface when
you're emulsifying something.

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:07.250
The surfactant has to come out of
the water and go to the interface.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:11.170
However, the surfactant does
not like to stay in the water,

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:14.620
because it has part of
it which is oil-like.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:16.580
It doesn't like to be in water.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:19.360
So instead, the surfactant
forms aggregates.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:21.160
We call these micelles.

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:26.200
The aggregate, so that the tail
groups, the oil part of the surfactants

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.150
are all connected together.

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:31.870
They're all facing one another,
excluding some of the water.

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:35.170
That allow the surfactant
to exist in the water

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:39.260
without exposing the tail
groups to the water itself.

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:42.280
But the micelles act as a reservoir.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:46.210
They can easily break up and bring
new surfactant to the interface

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:48.080
as you create the interface.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:49.780
And if you have enough surfactant--

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:53.320
an egg yolk, for example, is an
excellent surfactant-- they completely

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:55.930
coat all the interfaces.

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:59.040
And then when drops come
together, as I show here

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:02.830
in this little cartoon, when two drops
come together with a coated interface,

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:06.070
with a surfactant-covered
interface, it prevents the drops

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:09.160
from joining together and
becoming larger drops.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:13.470
So the drops remain stable, and
the emulsion remains stable.

